# Tessel page metadata design

Status: proposed implementation contract. `docs/brief.md` remains the source of truth for owner decisions. This design addresses open issues 233, 240, 247, and 251; it does not mark them fixed.

## Decisions

Tessel owns the outer document, so Tessel must put the page's identity into that document's head. A title or favicon inside the embedded HTML cannot reliably identify the browser tab or supply a link preview. Resolve metadata from the published HTML, store a revision-specific snapshot, and render the permitted fields into the wrapper's initial server response. Chat tools should never need to execute the page to get a card.

One HTML file remains sufficient. Standard HTML metadata is the optional customization interface; there is no required manifest, preview upload, or AI-generated summary. JavaScript pages get automatic extraction and a preview image through an isolated render of their published revision.

Visibility is checked before selecting metadata, including at image and icon endpoints. Bot detection is not an authorization mechanism.

## Visibility contract

| Surface | Public | Unlisted | Private |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Anonymous access | Full page | Full page with link | Generic access screen; no embedded content |
| Initial wrapper title and icon | Page identity | Page identity | `Tessel` and Tessel icon, including for signed-in requests |
| Authorized member's tab after access check | Page identity | Page identity | Page identity loaded through an authorized read |
| Link card | Page title, description, image | Same rich card as public | `Tessel`, generic product description, Tessel brand image |
| Search policy | `index,follow`; eligible for sitemap | `noindex,nofollow`; omitted from sitemap and Tessel discovery | `noindex,nofollow`; omitted from sitemap and discovery |
| Anonymous page-specific image/icon access | Allowed | Allowed with opaque URL | Denied, including old public URLs |

Unlisted means anyone with the link can read and unfurl the page. It is not confidential: a chat provider can fetch and cache its title, description, and image. Indexing directives are requests to compliant crawlers, not access controls. Explain this beside the visibility choice: “Anyone with the link can view this page. Shared links can show its title and preview.”

Private metadata must not identify the page, author, workspace, or members. The generic description is “Open pages on Tessel.” Use the same anonymous presentation for private, missing, and deleted page URLs, with a generic HTTP 404 response. A sign-in action can preserve the opaque same-origin destination without exposing its subject. A signed-in nonmember gets no additional identifying information. Shared cards always stay generic, even when a member pastes the link or requests the HTML with cookies.

## Resolving a page's identity

Resolve each field independently from the immutable published HTML revision. Draft edits do not change shared metadata until published. In the following precedence lists, skip empty or invalid candidates:

| Field | Precedence, highest first |
| --- | --- |
| Subject | Authored `<title>`; authored `og:title`; first meaningful authored `h1`; rendered document title; rendered `h1`; `Untitled page` |
| Description | Authored `meta[name=description]`; authored `og:description`; first meaningful paragraph in authored body; rendered description/paragraph; `An interactive page on Tessel.` |
| Icon | Authored `link[rel~=icon]`; rendered icon link; Tessel icon |
| Preview image | Valid authored `og:image`; valid authored `twitter:image`; automatic screenshot; generated title card |

“Authored” means present in the submitted source before scripts run. An explicit field in the HTML wins over inferred content. Do not infer a name from a filename, URL, account, or workspace. Do not discover a favicon by probing the publisher's domain. For multiple equally ranked tags use the first valid candidate in document order; choose a decodable icon with enough resolution for a 32px tab.

Read tags with an HTML parser, never regex. Decode entities, collapse whitespace, and emit escaped plain text. Ignore scripts, styles, templates, hidden content, navigation, and form values when deriving prose. Clip displayed titles to 120 characters and descriptions to 200 at word boundaries; retain the source and show the resulting text in the publisher's preview. Strip control characters. These are product limits, not promises about how much a sharing app will display. Do not summarize arbitrary paragraphs with an LLM.

Use `<subject> | Tessel` for the outer tab, `og:title`, and card title. Store the subject separately so the Tessel suffix is added exactly once. Keep the toolbar's Tessel identity visible even with a custom page icon. The publisher preview shows the chosen icon, tab title, description, and card, and identifies when a fallback is being used. Standard HTML tags can correct every automatic choice without leaving the single-file workflow.

For example, this optional head is sufficient:

```html
<head>
  <title>Friday lunch poll</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Choose where we eat this Friday.">
  <link rel="icon" href="data:image/png;base64,...">
  <!-- Optional; otherwise Tessel makes the image. -->
  <meta property="og:image" content="https://example.com/lunch.png">
</head>
```

Resolve relative asset URLs against the published document's asset base. Honor an authored `base` only for asset resolution after URL validation; it cannot change Tessel's canonical URL. Accept bounded data images and safe HTTPS assets. A referenced asset is optional; missing or rejected assets fall through to the next choice rather than blocking publication.

## Tabs and embedded documents

The outer wrapper has one metadata owner. It sets title and icon on first load and on client navigation. Reset them immediately when navigating to another page; do not leave the previous page's identity visible while loading. Verify that framework head updates cannot restore the default title afterward.

An authorized private viewer receives the stored identity through an authenticated, noncached read and applies only the tab title and icon locally. Do not insert private descriptions or social tags into the outer document, structured data, hydration payloads, or public APIs. On sign-out, membership revocation, or navigation, reset the tab to Tessel and remove the frame and private assets. Recheck access on resume from browser history and reconnect.

Live script changes such as `document.title = 'Results'` stay inside the frame in this first version. The wrapper uses the published metadata snapshot, including script-derived values captured at publication. This avoids a second live metadata protocol and keeps tabs and cards consistent. Republishing refreshes the snapshot. The sandboxed frame must never directly mutate the parent head or inject arbitrary head markup.

## Link card response

The canonical wrapper URL is the shared URL; never share an iframe, storage, or renderer URL. Preserve the existing route model. IDs and asset keys must be opaque and must not contain page titles, because any visibility may later become private.

The initial HTML contains:

- `<title>`, favicon links, and `meta[name=description]`.
- `og:type=website`, `og:site_name=Tessel`, `og:title`, `og:description`, `og:url`, and `og:image` with width, height, type, and useful image alt text.
- `twitter:card=summary_large_image` and explicit title, description, image, and image alt fields.
- A canonical URL and the visibility-appropriate robots directive, also applied as an HTTP `X-Robots-Tag` header to page-specific asset responses.

Use absolute HTTPS URLs from the configured canonical origin; never trust an incoming Host header or embedded `og:url`. Strip tracking and incidental query state from canonical metadata. A private fallback uses only generic text and the generic brand image; omit page-specific `og:url` and canonical tags from its anonymous error response. All fields, including image alt text and icon URLs, pass through the same visibility decision.

Return a complete usable card immediately, even while image capture is pending. A no-JavaScript client gets the subject, description, and a semantic link to open the interactive page. This supplies useful public identity without claiming to server-render arbitrary interactive HTML. A broader indexing strategy for JavaScript-only body content is separate from fixing blank link cards.

## Preview images and icons

Normalize preview images to a 1200 x 630 PNG. For an authored image, preserve its aspect ratio and letterbox rather than stretching it. Decode and re-encode fetched images; do not redirect a crawler to the author's URL. Copying validated bytes into Tessel storage makes the card stable and avoids third-party tracking on every unfurl. Custom icons are sanitized and rasterized into PNG sizes suitable for browser tabs and touch icons. Never serve publisher SVG or HTML directly from the wrapper's trusted origin. Tessel's own canonical icon supplies all default variants.

Automatic capture runs the page at a fixed 1200 x 630 viewport with consistent fonts, light color scheme, reduced motion, and a fresh anonymous browser context. Capture the embedded content, excluding the Tessel toolbar. Let JavaScript render, wait for the DOM and available fonts, then allow at most two seconds of settling within a ten-second total job budget. Stop animations and capture once; do not wait forever for network idle, click anything, scroll through flows, or simulate votes. The same render can collect fallback title and prose for JavaScript-built pages.

If capture fails, times out, or yields no meaningful visible content, use a deterministic title card with the resolved subject, available description, Tessel mark, and Tessel colors. This card is page-specific and immediately available without a browser job. A screenshot of an error or sign-in screen is not a successful preview. Private links always use the generic Tessel image; no private screenshots are generated for sharing.

Publish-time static parsing supplies the initial snapshot and title card. At most one browser job per revision fills missing metadata and captures an image when needed. Its completion atomically publishes a new metadata version. The publishing interface offers “Preview ready,” “Preparing preview,” or “Using title card,” with “Regenerate preview” after failure. Publishing and reading still work if capture fails. Republish or an explicit publisher action retries expensive work; crawler traffic never triggers rendering.

## Rendering cannot perform page actions

Issue 251 requires a runtime boundary, not a special crawler user agent. The renderer must never open the live wrapper as a member or owner.

1. Run the immutable HTML revision in a disposable, isolated browser/container with no Tessel cookies, API keys, session, local storage, service workers, or access to host files and internal networks.
2. Provide a preview-mode runtime adapter with empty, deterministic read results and no write capability. Every write method returns a controlled “Unavailable in preview” result. Do not mint a live runtime credential. The runtime backend independently rejects preview principals and missing credentials on every mutation.
3. Deny browser network egress by default, including fetch, XHR, WebSocket, beacons, forms, navigation, workers, downloads, and image URL tricks. Serve only the source artifact and prevalidated static dependencies from a job-local asset bundle. Block newly requested or dynamically constructed external URLs. Blocking POST alone is insufficient: a GET can also cause side effects.
4. Resolve dependencies through a bounded static-asset fetcher before execution. Accept public HTTPS destinations only; block credentials, private/loopback/link-local addresses, cloud metadata endpoints, and Tessel action/auth/runtime URLs. Recheck DNS and every redirect, enforce MIME/byte/time limits, and send no cookies or authorization. Cache safe image/font/style/script bytes for the job. Do not follow arbitrary links or execute assets during collection.
5. Give jobs CPU, memory, dependency-count, byte, and wall-clock limits. Deduplicate and cap jobs per publisher and rate-limit asset serving. A script that hangs or tries a forbidden dependency gets a title card.

A dependency fetch is still an external GET, so no renderer can promise that a publisher-controlled third-party server has no side effects. Tessel guarantees that capture cannot write Tessel page data or run with a reader's authority. Reject known action URLs and document the residual external-fetch limitation; authors must use static asset URLs. Pages requiring live data or unrestricted network access may have less representative screenshots and can supply `og:image` instead.

The public wrapper and all metadata/asset GET and HEAD handlers are read-only. For ordinary embedded browsing, the runtime must require a valid page-scoped session and explicit user activation before permitting mutations; loading a page alone cannot cast a vote. Implement this in the trusted runtime boundary, not an author-set `preview=true` flag. A runtime capability can be enabled by a trusted interaction gate outside the untrusted frame; anonymous voting must not require sign-in, but it still requires that interaction. This is a proposed change to automatic-on-load writes and needs compatibility communication. Metadata crawlers never enter that interactive path. Do not claim this proves a human acted or prevents deliberate automated voting.

## Storage, visibility changes, and races

The database owns visibility, published revision, resolved metadata with field provenance, selected image/icon keys, metadata version, and job state. HTML and generated bytes live in object storage. A stored snapshot is a derived cache of a specific source revision, not another editable source of truth.

Keep asset buckets private. Public delivery goes through Tessel's asset gateway using opaque page/revision/metadata-version identifiers. Every request, including an old image URL, checks current page visibility before releasing bytes. An authenticated private icon read uses the member's permission and `Cache-Control: private, no-store`; no anonymous signed URL may bypass the check. Original private HTML and its assets require the same protection.

Initially serve wrapper HTML and page-derived assets with `Cache-Control: no-store`, with no shared CDN cache or stale-on-error behavior. This trades some performance for immediate origin revocation. Internal byte caches may sit behind the visibility gate. The generic Tessel brand assets alone can be cached publicly for a long time. Any future shared caching requires an explicit revocation design before enabling it.

On visibility change or deletion, atomically update the visibility generation and stop anonymous delivery first. Cancel obsolete jobs, remove ineligible sitemap/discovery entries, and retire generated assets through bounded cleanup. Jobs carry page ID, owner ID, revision, and visibility generation; recheck owner liveness, current visibility, revision, and generation before rendering and before committing or exposing results. A job started while public cannot publish its result after the page becomes private. Private-to-public publication creates a fresh permitted snapshot and version before rich metadata is served.

Public-to-unlisted keeps rich previews but immediately removes discovery eligibility and adds `noindex`. Public/unlisted-to-private switches every anonymous surface to the generic fallback and denies previous derived image/icon URLs. Deletion does the same for access and schedules byte cleanup. Register object keys before writing bytes so cleanup can find objects left by cancelled or failed jobs.

Tessel cannot erase a card, screenshot, browser history entry, or downloaded image already held by a third party. Show this limitation when changing a previously accessible page to private. Never promise retroactive secrecy. Versioned image URLs refresh future fetches, but many chat tools cache cards by page URL and may need their own refresh; do not change the canonical page URL to work around that cache.

## Assumptions made while the owner is away

- Published revisions are the unit of sharing; if the current product has only immediate saves, introduce an immutable revision per save rather than exposing mutable draft metadata.
- Standard tags embedded in HTML are sufficient customization for this release. Separate metadata settings and manifests would create competing sources and are deferred.
- A private member may see the page's identity in their own browser tab after authorization. This local convenience does not authorize a rich private chat preview.
- Unlisted pages intentionally have rich cards. There is no separate “hide preview” switch in this version.
- A screenshot is the default automatic image; a title card is the safe fallback. Data-dependent pages may need an authored image for an exact representation.
- Live script changes do not update the outer tab until republished. This can be revisited with a narrowly scoped, authenticated frame bridge if real workflows need it.
- Preventing on-load runtime writes takes precedence over preserving that behavior. A trusted interaction gate is part of fixing issue 251, with no new sign-in requirement for public polls.
- No new domain, hosting framework, external AI service, or preview vendor is needed to adopt this contract. Infrastructure selection and rollout remain implementation work.

## Acceptance checks for implementation

| Bug or boundary | Required evidence |
| --- | --- |
| 233: private title leak | Request a private page as anonymous, bot, member, and nonmember. Initial HTML, headers, social tags, asset URLs, alt text, and serialized data reveal none of its subject or paragraph. Its card is generic. An authorized member's tab later gets the correct identity. |
| 240: default tab identity | Publish HTML with a title and data-URL icon. Verify outer tabs on reload, client navigation, back/forward, and narrow tabs. Verify private member tabs and reset on logout/revocation. |
| 247: blank JavaScript card | Publish a page whose heading and content are built by JavaScript. After processing, a plain HTTP fetch contains resolved metadata and a reachable 1200 x 630 image without executing JavaScript. Test timeout and missing-assets fallbacks too. |
| 251: phantom votes | A poll attempts writes on load using runtime methods, fetch, beacon, WebSocket, and GET URLs. Repeated capture, metadata fetch, and real chat unfurls leave live data unchanged. A deliberate ordinary viewer interaction can still vote. |
| Unlisted policy | Anonymous card is specific; robots directives exclude indexing; sitemap/discovery omit the page; asset URLs do not expose names. |
| Revocation and races | Fetch old card/icon URLs after making a page private or deleting it; content is denied. Finish an in-flight old job afterward; it cannot restore metadata or serve bytes. |
| Hostile input | Test injected head markup, invalid/oversized images, active SVG, redirect/DNS SSRF, malicious scripts, and infinite rendering; metadata stays escaped and jobs remain isolated and bounded. |
| Real sharing behavior | Paste public, unlisted, and private URLs into representative chat/social tools while signed out. Confirm title, icon, description, image, fallback, and refresh behavior; record external cache limitations. |

This document is a design only. No runtime behavior or bug status changes until these checks pass in the implemented system.
